  "client_view_text": " This is a key innovation of the atomic commit protocol that makes it possible to ensure consistency and durability in the database, even in the presence of crashes and failures.\n### Step 1: Understand the concept of a write-ahead log\nA write-ahead log is a data structure that stores a record of all the changes made to the database. It's called \"write-ahead\" because the log entries are written to disk before the actual changes are made to the database.\n\n### Step 2: Explain how the write-ahead log helps with crash recovery\nWhen a database crashes, the log can be used to recover the database to a consistent state by replaying the committed transactions from the log. This involves reading the log entries from disk, applying the changes recorded in each log entry to the database, and discarding any changes that were not committed at the time of the crash.\n\n### Step 3: Describe the role of atomic commitment in ensuring consistency and durability\nThe atomic commitment protocol ensures that either all or none of the changes made by a transaction are written to the log and the database. This means that if a crash occurs after a transaction has been partially committed (i.e., some but not all of its changes have been written to the log and the database), the transaction can be safely"
